- How would you reconstruct the actual windows efficiently and handle negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
DP over subsequences with a fixed length captures real-world problems like quantizing sequences while minimizing total variation; it tests state definition, reconstruction, and complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: last index and length; - Transition: O(n) over prior indices; - Reconstruction via parent pointers; - Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubsequence(A, k) {
  const n = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, last = -1;
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (dp[i][k] < best) { best = dp[i][k]; last = i; }
  const idx = Array(k);
  let t = k, cur = last;
  while (t>=1) { idx[t-1] = cur; cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## How It Works

Concise technical explanation of the mechanism.

## Key Points

- **DP State**: local[k], global[k] track ending vs non-ending subarrays
- **Transition**: local[k] = max(local[k]+a[i], global[k-1]+a[i]); global[k] = max(global[k], local[k])
- **Edge Handling**: cap K to n; initialize -inf; handles negative values

## Example

```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, K) {
  const n = A.length;
  if (n === 0 || K <= 0) return 0;
  K = Math.min(K, n);
  const global = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  const local = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  global[0] = 0;
  for (let x of A) {
    for (let k = K; k >= 1; k--) {
      local[k] = Math.max((local[k] !== -Infinity ? local[k] + x : -Infinity), global[k-1] + x);
      global[k] = Math.max(global[k], local[k]);
    }
  }
  return global[K];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: partitioning a string into k segments with a per-segment distinct-character score, a practical proxy for text analytics. It tests state formulation, precomputation, and reconstruction, with a baseline O(n^2k) DP suitable for beginner to intermediate levels.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitioning problems
- Precomputing substring properties in O(n^2)
- Reconstructing cuts via parent pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxScorePartition(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    const seen = new Set();
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      seen.add(s[j]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction could be added here
  return {score: dp[n][k], cutStartIndices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) and compute dist on the fly?
- How does the approach change if k can be 0 or n+? and if n is large?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP with exactly k block selection, reconstruction, and non-adjacent constraint handling. Requires precomputing interval values (max-min) and a clean end-at-i transition that accounts for the gap requirement.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP with exactly k non-adjacent blocks
- Precomputation of val(l,r) = max(A[l..r]) - min(A[l..r]) using O(n²) sliding window enumeration
- Reconstruction pointers to retrieve endpoints
- Time complexity: O(n²) precomputation + O(kn²) DP; Space: O(n²) for val table + O(n·k) for DP
- Edge cases: k > ⌊n/2⌋ (impossible, return error), empty arrays (return 0)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSumIntervals(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const val = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let min = A[l], max = A[l];
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      min = Math.min(min, A[r]);
      max = Math.max(max, A[r]);
      val[l][r] = max - min;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const prev = j >= 2 ? dp[j-2][t-1] : (t === 1 ? 0 : -Infinity);
        if (prev === -Infinity) continue;
        const curr = prev + val[j-1][i-1];
        if (curr > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = curr;
          parent[i][t] = { start: j, end: i, prev: j - 2 };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && parent[i][t]) {
    const p = parent[i][t];
    if (!p.skip) {
      intervals.push([p.start, p.end]);
      i = p.prev;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], intervals: intervals.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests substring DP and reconstruction in a practical string-cleanup task, common in data processing pipelines at scale.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings: dp[i][j] captures minimal deletions for s[i..j]
- Reconstruction: recover exact deletions and resulting palindrome
- Complexity: O(n^2) time and O(n^2) space; can be optimized with path tracking

## Code Example
```javascript
function minDeletionsPalindrome(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j]) dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j-1];
      else dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]) + 1;
    }
  }
  return {deletions: dp[0][n-1], dp};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reconstruct the actual palindrome after deletions?
- How to optimize to O(n) space while keeping reconstruction capability?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of dynamic programming on prefixes with a non-crossing pairing constraint. The core challenge is encoding the no-crossing condition by considering the last pair ending at position i with its left partner p.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefixes**: Extra dimension tracks the number of pairs formed
- **Non-crossing constraint**: Enforced by pairing i with p < i and using dp[p-1][t-1], which ensures all pairs lie completely to the left of p
- **Reconstruction**: Requires storing backpointers to output the actual pairs
- **Complexity**: Time O(n²k), Space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPairs(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      prev[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let p = 1; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p - 1][t - 1] + A[p - 1] * A[i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skip: false, p };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t].skip) {
      i--;
    } else {
      const p = prev[i][t].p;
      pairs.push([p, i]);
      i = p - 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs: pairs.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem introduces a dynamic programming angle where subarrays are weighted by their block index. It tests the ability to transform interval sums into prefix sums, derive a two-dimensional DP over position and block count, and implement solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) interval sum computation
- 2D DP over position and block count
- Solution reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k); Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScorePartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = [0];
  for (let x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1] + x);
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = P[i];
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
        const score = dp[p][t-1] + (P[i] - P[p]) * t;
        if (score > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = score;
          parent[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct partitions
  const partitions = [];
  let end = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = parent[end][t];
    partitions.unshift([start+1, end]);
    end = start;
  }

  return { maxScore: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

